<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galeazzo Ciano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galeazzo Ciano was an Italian Fascist politician, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>'s son-in-law and foreign minister from 1936 to 1943. His diaries are a major source for Fascist foreign policy. In July 1943 he voted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> against Mussolini, who was then deposed; he was later tried by the Italian Social Republic at Verona and shot in January 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galeazzo-ciano.jpg" alt="Galeazzo Ciano" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galeazzo_Ciano01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Galeazzo Ciano was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> on 18 March 1903, the son of Admiral Costanzo Ciano, a hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-fascist-party">National Fascist Party</a> and the reorganiser of the Italian merchant navy in the 1920s. Father and son both took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s March on Rome in 1922. Galeazzo studied the philosophy of law at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, worked briefly as a journalist and then entered the diplomatic service. On 24 April 1930 he married Benito Mussolini's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Edda</a>, and they had three children. He then served at the Italian Legation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and as consul in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1935 Ciano became minister of press and propaganda. He volunteered for the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> as a bomber squadron commander and won two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> medals of valour. In 1936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> made him foreign minister, and he began his diary soon afterwards. He negotiated the formal alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-steel">Pact of Steel</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in May 1939.</p><p>In early August 1939 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to explain that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was not ready for a large war. When Italy declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1940 he wrote in his diary, &quot;I am sad, very sad. The adventure begins. May God help Italy!&quot;</p><p>Ciano became disenchanted with Nazi Germany, but he went along with the ill-advised parallel war and the badly executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> he pressed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s exit from the war, and on 5 February 1943 he was removed from the foreign ministry with the rest of the cabinet. He then became ambassador to the Holy See, presenting his credentials to Pope Pius XII on 1 March 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 24 July 1943 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-of-fascism">Fascist Grand Council</a> voted 19 to 8 for Dino Grandi's resolution asking the king to resume his constitutional powers, and Ciano voted in favour. The king dismissed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> the next day and had him arrested. Ciano and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Edda</a> fled with their children to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 27 August 1943. The Germans, furious at his vote, turned him over to Mussolini's Italian Social Republic, which arrested him for treason. After the Verona trial he was shot on 11 January 1944 with four others who had voted against Mussolini, all tied to chairs.</p><p>His diaries of 1937 to 1943, survived because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Edda</a> smuggled five of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. They were first published in English in 1946 and were used by the prosecution against Joachim von Ribbentrop at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> trials.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Galeazzo Ciano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Verona Manifesto of 1943 promised a socialist-style programme, and the Verona trial of January 1944 executed <b>Galeazzo Ciano</b> and others who had voted against Mussolini.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-social-republic">Italian Social Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Verona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.55,10.32&amp;year=1929" aria-label="Livorno on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4798 620 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Galeazzo Ciano"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5248 890h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5248 890h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.55,10.32&amp;year=1929">What was here in 1929? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
